{"name":"Sea Glass","hex":"#afc1bf","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sea glass","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Rounded glass pebbles originating from glassware products that were lost at sea and broken up, which are found on beaches and have been rounded and aged through the action of surf."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sea_glass"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sea glass","blocks":[{"text":"Sea glass is physically polished and chemically weathered glass found on beaches along bodies of salt water. It consists of fragments of drinkwares or other human-made vessels, which often have the appearance of tumbled stones. Weathering produces natural frosted glass. Sea glass is used for decoration, most commonly in jewelry. Beach glass is physically polished and chemically weathered glass found on beaches along bodies of fresh water and is often less frosted in appearance than sea glass. Sea glass takes 20–40 years, and sometimes as much as 100–200 years, to acquire its characteristic texture and shape. It is also colloquially referred to as drift glass from the longshore drift process that forms the smooth edges. In practice, the two terms are used interchangeably."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3e/Amant.JPG/330px-Amant.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea_glass","wikidata":"Q3117962"},{"title":"Sea Glass (novel)","blocks":[{"text":"Sea Glass is a 2002 romance novel by Anita Shreve, first published on April 9, 2022 by Little, Brown and Company. It is chronologically the second novel in Shreve's informal trilogy to be set in a large beach house on the New Hampshire coast that used to be a convent. It is preceded by Fortune's Rocks and followed by The Pilot's Wife."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/9/96/Seaglassbook.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea_Glass_(novel)","wikidata":"Q7439905"},{"title":"Sea","blocks":[{"text":"A sea is a very large body of salty water. The term has two distinct meanings. The first is the interconnected body of seawater that spans most of Earth, known as the sea or the ocean. The second is a named body of water: usually either a marginal sea, or a large, nearly landlocked body of water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg/330px-Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea","wikidata":"Q165"},{"title":"Glass","blocks":[{"text":"Glass is an amorphous (non-crystalline) solid. Because it is often transparent and chemically inert, glass has found widespread practical, technological, and decorative use in window panes, tableware, and optics. Some common objects made of glass are named after the material, e.g., a \"glass\" for drinking, \"glasses\" for vision correction, and a \"magnifying glass\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/43/Fassade_Wilhelmstrasse_65%2C_Berlin-Mitte%2C_160417%2C_ako.jpg/330px-Fassade_Wilhelmstrasse_65%2C_Berlin-Mitte%2C_160417%2C_ako.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Glass","wikidata":"Q11469"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Sea_glass_-_01.jpg/500px-Sea_glass_-_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Sea_glass_-_01.jpg/1280px-Sea_glass_-_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Sea_glass_-_01.jpg/500px-Sea_glass_-_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Assorted colors of sea glass","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ASea_glass_-_01.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Black_glass_sea_glass_%28cropped%29.JPG/500px-Black_glass_sea_glass_%28cropped%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1b/Black_glass_sea_glass_%28cropped%29.JPG/500px-Black_glass_sea_glass_%28cropped%29.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"\"Black\" sea glass collected in Jamaica. Under good lighting the green shade of the original glass before weathering can be seen.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlack_glass_sea_glass_(cropped).JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/BlueMarble-2001-2002.jpg/500px-BlueMarble-2001-2002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/BlueMarble-2001-2002.jpg/1280px-BlueMarble-2001-2002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/BlueMarble-2001-2002.jpg/500px-BlueMarble-2001-2002.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Composite images of the Earth created by NASA in 2001","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABlueMarble-2001-2002.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Propagation_du_tsunami_en_profondeur_variable.gif/330px-Propagation_du_tsunami_en_profondeur_variable.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2d/Propagation_du_tsunami_en_profondeur_variable.gif/330px-Propagation_du_tsunami_en_profondeur_variable.gif?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"When the wave enters sh